<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#qatabanian-language">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qatabanian language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qatabanic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qatabanian is an Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> language spoken in the ancient kingdom of Qataban in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and written in the South Arabian script. It is known from several hundred inscriptions of roughly the 5th century BCE to the 2nd century CE, including royal decrees, dedications and the market law of Timna. It is closely related to Sabaic, Minaic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadramautic-language">Hadramitic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qatabanian-language.jpg" alt="Qatabanian language" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qatabanic_Inscription_SE_60.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
